Local energy production decentralizes power systems, builds community resilience, and reduces transmission losses inherent in centralized grids. Products and manufacturing systems designed to leverage local renewable energy become more resilient to supply disruptions while reducing grid demands. This strategy creates opportunities for distributed manufacturing and repair ecosystems that operate independently of centralized energy infrastructure. From a circular economy perspective, local energy production supports the principle of operating within bioregional boundaries and enables communities to have direct control over sustainability outcomes.